    <heading level="1">Practice Makes Perfect</heading>
    <paragraph>Graph Quadratic Functions of the form $$f(x) = x^{2} + k$$</paragraph>
    <paragraph>In the following exercises, ⓐ graph the quadratic functions on the same rectangular coordinate system and ⓑ describe what effect adding a constant, k, to the function has on the basic parabola.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="293" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>$$f(x) = x^{2}, g(x) = x^{2} + 4,$$ and $$h(x) = x^{2} - 4.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>ⓐ</paragraph>
        <paragraph>ⓑ The graph of $$g(x)=x2+4g(x)=x2+4$$ is the same as the graph of $$f(x)=x2f(x)=x2$$ but shifted up 4 units. The graph of $$h(x)=x2-4h(x)=x2-4$$ is the same as the graph of $$f(x)=x2f(x)=x2$$ but shift down 4 units.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
